#original-page(595pt, 842pt, none)[
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 9.12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(295.4pt, 780pt, "2 ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(90pt, 88.56pt, "1. Introduction ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(114pt, 113.04pt, "Efficient thermal management remains a key bottleneck in wide-bandgap (WBG) ")
  #line-at(90pt, 137.52pt, "and ultra-wide-bandgap (UWBG) electronic devices, where self-heating limits ")
  #line-at(90pt, 162pt, "reliability, output power, and scaling potential [1–3]. A common strategy to mitigate ")
  #line-at(90pt, 186.24pt, "these issues is heterogeneous integration, which involves bonding active layers to high-")
  #line-at(90pt, 210.72pt, "thermal-conductivity substrates, such as diamond [4,5] or SiC [6,7], or inserting ")
  #line-at(90pt, 235.2pt, "intermediate buffer layers to spread heat laterally. Yet, in these complex stacks, heat ")
  #line-at(90pt, 259.68pt, "dissipation is often governed not by the bulk materials but by the buried thermal ")
  #line-at(90pt, 284.16pt, "boundary conductance (TBC) at nonmetal–nonmetal interfaces, where phonon ")
  #line-at(90pt, 308.64pt, "coupling is intrinsically poor. Despite their technological importance, quantitative ")
  #line-at(90pt, 333.12pt, "studies of buried TBC remain limited: fewer than 50 interfaces have been ")
  #line-at(90pt, 357.6pt, "systematically characterized [8], and most reported data concern metal–nonmetal ")
  #line-at(90pt, 381.84pt, "interfaces measured by time-domain thermoreflectance (TDTR) [9–11]. ")
  #line-at(114pt, 406.32pt, "Pump–probe thermoreflectance methods such as TDTR and frequency-domain ")
  #line-at(90pt, 430.8pt, "thermoreflectance (FDTR) [12–14] have become indispensable for microscale thermal ")
  #line-at(90pt, 455.28pt, "characterization. By modulating a focused pump laser and detecting reflectance ")
  #line-at(90pt, 479.76pt, "changes either as a function of delay time or modulation frequency, these techniques ")
  #line-at(90pt, 504.24pt, "offer non-contact access to thermal conductivity and TBC. Conventional TDTR, ")
  #line-at(90pt, 528.72pt, "however, typically operates at modulation frequencies of 0.1-10 MHz, giving thermal ")
  #line-at(90pt, 553.2pt, "penetration depths of only a few microns [15]. This limitation restricts its sensitivity to ")
  #line-at(90pt, 577.44pt, "shallow interfaces and makes parameter extraction in multilayer systems nontrivial. ")
  #line-at(90pt, 601.92pt, "FDTR can access a broader frequency range, but unlike time-domain approaches that ")
  #line-at(90pt, 626.4pt, "directly sample the transient response, FDTR’s interpretation depends on frequency-")
  #line-at(90pt, 650.88pt, "domain modeling and accurate calibration of the phase reference and system transfer ")
  #line-at(90pt, 675.36pt, "function, which can complicate robust multiparameter inversion in complex multilayers.  ")
  #line-at(114pt, 699.84pt, "Recent developments have sought to overcome these depth and robustness ")
  #line-at(90pt, 724.32pt, "constraints. Steady-state thermoreflectance (SSTR) achieves low-frequency heating ")
  #line-at(90pt, 748.8pt, "suitable for buried substrates several micrometers thick [16]. Dual-modulation TDTR ")
]

